Scope of Use
Branch Management (Rebase)


use git rebaseTo rearrange the commits into a straight line.
Simplify the commit history and make the merge cleaner.
Temporary Work (Stash)


use git stashTo save unfinished work without committing.
Ideal for switching branches without losing unfinished work.
Version Control (Tag)


use git tagTo create important reference points, such as releases or milestones.
This makes it easy to revert to a specific version.

✅ Checklist for advanced Git usage
[ ] use git rebaseTo make the commit history clean and easy to read.
[ ] use git stashWhen there are pending tasks and you need to switch branches.
[ ] use git tagTo define a significant milestone or release.
[ ] Check commit history after rebase to prevent data loss.
[ ] Push tag to remote so that the team can access it.
[ ] Sync Docs/Wiki after major updates.

⚖️ Principles of Use
use foxExercise caution, especially when working with a team (you should rebase your own branch, not a shared branch).
use stashThis is for temporary storage only and should not be used as a substitute for a complete commit.
use tagTo create clear points of reference, such as releases or milestones.
Maintain transparency in commit and tag logs.
Respect safety and ethical considerations when sharing information.

